  "citations": {
    "mop_2012": "Moskowitz, Ooi & Pedersen (2012), 'Time Series Momentum', Journal of Financial Economics 104(2):228-250",
    "section_2_1_construction": "We construct a return series for each instrument as follows. Each day, we compute the daily excess return of the most liquid futures contract (typically the nearest or next nearest-to-delivery contract), and then compound the daily returns to a cumulative return index from which we can compute returns at any horizon.",
    "section_6_3_decomposition": "Futures return_{t-12,t} = Price change_{t-12,t} + Roll return_{t-12,t}",
    "section_6_3_magnitude": "In financial futures with little storage costs or convenience yield, the roll return is close to zero, but, in commodity markets, the roll return can be substantial.",
    "section_6_3_price_basis": "where prices are measured as the nearest-to-expiration futures price",
    "far_contract_robustness": "As a robustness test, we also use the 'far' futures contract (the next maturity after the most liquid one). For the commodity futures, time series momentum profits are in fact slightly stronger for the far contract, and, for the financial futures, time series momentum returns hardly change if we use far futures.",
    "equity_cash_correlation": "For the equity indexes, our return series are almost perfectly correlated with the corresponding returns of the underlying cash indexes in excess of the Treasury bill rate.",
    "eq1_ex_ante_volatility_not_implemented_here": "sigma_t^2 = 261 * sum_{i=0..inf} (1-delta) delta^i (r_{t-1-i} - rbar_t)^2, with delta chosen so that the centre of mass of the weights, sum (1-delta) delta^i i = delta/(1-delta), is 60 days (MOP 2012 Eq. 1, p.234)"
  },
